      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="natural-deodorants">
        <h2>Natural Deodorant Ingredients and Regulatory Considerations</h2>
        <p>
          The natural deodorant segment has grown significantly in response to consumer demand for aluminium-free and synthetic-preservative-free underarm products. It is important to distinguish two separate consumer concerns that drive this demand: a preference for ingredients of natural origin, and a specific aversion to aluminium salts. The former drives the formulation of deodorants using naturally derived antibacterial actives; the latter is a product category distinction — any product without an aluminium salt active is not an antiperspirant by definition, regardless of how effective it may be at controlling odour. Natural deodorant formulations cannot make antiperspirant claims or position themselves as sweat-reduction products without aluminium salt actives.
        </p>
        <p>
          The most technically effective natural deodorant actives are sodium bicarbonate — which raises axillary skin surface pH from approximately 6.5–7 toward 8–9, inhibiting the acid-producing Corynebacterium species responsible for the majority of volatile fatty acid malodour — and magnesium hydroxide, which provides a similar alkalising and mild antibacterial effect with substantially lower skin irritation risk than high-concentration bicarbonate. Arrowroot powder and non-GMO tapioca starch are included as physical moisture absorbers that reduce the sensation of dampness without occluding glands. Essential oils (Australian tea tree oil, lavender, eucalyptus) provide fragrance and mild antimicrobial activity from terpenoid and phenolic components, but have low skin substantivity and short-duration antibacterial effect on the underarm microbiome. Crystal deodorants — blocks or sprays of potassium alum (KAl(SO₄)₂·12H₂O) or ammonium alum — provide aluminium ions that inhibit some odour-causing bacteria, but the aluminium species in alum does not form the gel plug required for sweat duct occlusion; they are not effective antiperspirants. The full spectrum of natural cosmetic ingredient strategy — including preservative systems, emulsifier selection, and stability assessment for natural positioning — is covered in our resource on <a href="/how-cosmetic-formulations-are-developed" target="_top">cosmetic formulation development</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          From a regulatory standpoint, natural deodorant products in the EU must comply with EU Cosmetics Regulation 1223/2009 including the restricted substance lists in Annexes II–VI, which do not exempt naturally derived ingredients from restriction — a plant-derived antimicrobial that appears in Annex II is prohibited regardless of its origin. Marketing claims such as "aluminium-free" require careful substantiation: the claim is factually accurate only if the formulation contains no aluminium compounds in any form, including alum. Claims implying equivalent efficacy to antiperspirant products without clinical substantiation risk being misleading under EU Directive 2005/29/EC on unfair commercial practices and equivalent national consumer protection legislation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between an antiperspirant and a deodorant?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">An antiperspirant reduces sweat volume by physically occluding the underarm sweat duct using aluminium salt chemistry — the aluminium cation forms a gel plug in the keratin-lined duct that mechanically restricts sweat flow. A deodorant does not affect sweat volume; it acts on the underarm microbiome to inhibit bacterial production of volatile malodorous compounds from apocrine secretions, typically supplemented with fragrance to mask residual odour. In regulatory terms, antiperspirants are OTC drugs in the US (21 CFR Part 350) and cosmetics in the EU — the regulatory split means products must be assessed separately for each market.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does aluminium chlorohydrate block sweat glands?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Aluminium chlorohydrate (ACH) hydrolyses at the near-neutral pH of the upper eccrine duct to form polynuclear aluminium hydroxide species. These cationic aluminium complexes adsorb electrostatically onto the negatively charged keratin proteins lining the duct wall, forming a physical gel plug that occludes the duct lumen and reduces sweat output. The plug is not permanent — it disperses with washing, dilution by sweat, and mechanical friction. Application to dry skin is essential: pre-existing sweat dilutes ACH concentration before the gel forms, significantly reducing efficacy. Application to dry skin in the evening allows plug formation overnight for maximum next-day performance.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Are aluminium-based antiperspirants safe according to regulatory authorities?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The SCCS Opinion 1613/19 (2020) found aluminium in cosmetics safe at a total systemic exposure limit of 0.6 mg/kg body weight per day from all cosmetic sources combined. Topical absorption of aluminium through intact axillary skin is very low. The hypothesised causal link between aluminium antiperspirant use and breast cancer was explicitly assessed by the SCCS and found not to be supported by available epidemiological or mechanistic evidence. The US FDA likewise regards aluminium chlorohydrate and aluminium-zirconium complexes as safe and effective OTC drug actives at the specified concentrations under the antiperspirant monograph.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What antibacterial actives are used in deodorants and how do they work?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The primary deodorant actives currently in use are: triethyl citrate (TEC), which inhibits bacterial lipase enzyme activity — preventing enzymatic release of malodorous free fatty acids from apocrine ester precursors; zinc ricinoleate, which traps volatile malodour molecules via coordination chemistry; silver-based actives (silver citrate, silver zeolite), which release Ag⁺ ions inhibiting bacterial metalloenzymes; and ethanol above 60%, which denatures bacterial surface proteins. Triclosan — historically the most effective deodorant antibacterial — is now restricted in EU cosmetics and effectively absent from new EU-compliant deodorant formulations.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do natural deodorants work without aluminium compounds?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Natural deodorants without aluminium cannot reduce sweat volume — they address only the odour component. Sodium bicarbonate raises axillary pH to inhibit acid-producing Corynebacterium species responsible for volatile fatty acid malodour; magnesium hydroxide provides a similar alkalising effect with lower irritation risk. Arrowroot and tapioca starch absorb surface moisture physically. Essential oils (tea tree, lavender) provide mild, short-duration antimicrobial activity. Crystal deodorants (potassium alum, ammonium alum) release aluminium ions that inhibit some bacteria but do not form the gel plug required for sweat gland occlusion — they are not effective antiperspirants despite containing aluminium.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How are antiperspirants regulated differently from deodorants globally?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">In the US, antiperspirants are OTC drugs under FDA 21 CFR Part 350 — they require FDA-approved actives (ACH, Al-Zr glycine complexes), drug manufacturing GMP compliance, and drug facts panel labelling. Deodorants without antiperspirant claims are US cosmetics. In the EU and UK, both categories are cosmetics under EU Regulation 1223/2009 — aluminium salts are listed in Annex III (restricted, with maximum concentrations). In Canada, antiperspirants are regulated as natural health products or drugs. This regulatory divergence requires separate compliance pathways for products intended for both US and EU markets, even when the formulation is identical.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

